  "body": "## The report\n\n> the DB or analytics page seems to not like show more than 50040 requests? I left it running overnight doing a ton of api requests and I didn't see requests number or token usage increase lifetime for hy3\n\nBoth symptoms, one mechanism. Nothing was broken.\n\n`REQUEST_LOG_MAX_ROWS` defaults to **50,000**; prune runs every 100 inserts, so the count oscillates between 50,000 and 50,099. Every figure in `stats()` is a `COUNT(*)`/`SUM()` over that same table, so **at the cap the totals converge to a constant** — one row leaves for each one that arrives.\n\nEvidence from the reporter's live database (1.7 GB, read-only):\n\n```\nrows now   : 50,073\noldest row : 2026-07-31 12:10 UTC    <- mid-day cut, i.e. actively deleting\n2026-07-31 :  1,638 rows             <- partial day, being eaten\n2026-08-08 :  9,266 rows\n```\n\n~9,000 requests/day ⇒ a **5.5-day window**. There was no cumulative counter anywhere in the codebase, so \"lifetime\" meant \"the last 50,000 requests\" — and neither the UI nor the docs said so.\n\n## What this adds\n\n- **`request_totals`** — one row per `(day, provider, model)`, incremented at flush, never pruned. Only ids not already stored are folded in, since the insert is `INSERT OR REPLACE` and the counters are add-only.\n- **Backfill**, once, *before the writer accepts its first flush* so no request is counted by both the rollup and the aggregate. **1.0 s on the real 1.7 GB / 50k-row database**, no measurable file growth. Rows already pruned are gone and are not invented.\n- **`server_sessions`** — the writer records when a server was actually running. The same report asked about a 9-hour overnight hole; a flat chart cannot distinguish *no traffic* from *no server*, and now it can.\n- **UI** — an `All time` section, an explicit notice when stored rows are at the cap, and an uptime line under the series chart whose gap threshold scales with the selected range so a restart does not cry wolf.\n- **`clear()` erases the counters too** — \"Clear log\" is an explicit erase, and millions of all-time requests over an empty table reads as a bug.\n\n## Verification\n\nAgainst a consistent copy (SQLite backup API — a plain `cp` of a live database tears mid-write and I hit that) of the reporter's real store:\n\n| | windowed | all time | hy3 all-time |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| before | 50,032 | 50,032 | 12,936 req |\n| after 2,000 more | **50,032** | **52,032** | **14,936 req** |\n\nThat is the reported bug, reproduced and fixed on the reporter's own data.\n\nDriven in a real browser against a server serving that 1.7 GB store: cards, model table, retention notice and uptime line all render; **no console errors**; contrast **7.30–17.97:1** (all pass AA); the uptime threshold behaves correctly at 1h/24h/7d; and at a 492px viewport the new table is the only analytics table that **does not** overflow — the 8-column provider/key tables already did, which is pre-existing and untouched.\n\nTests: 22 new (13 store, 6 endpoint, 3 docs-contract). Local suite **3,374 passed**, with the 6 known PowerShell installer failures that predate this branch.\n\n## Docs\n\nREADME, `docs/USAGE.md` and the in-dashboard Guide all now explain the cap, what All time means, that clearing erases it, and the disk trade-off (~30 KB/row, so 50k ≈ 1.7 GB). A new contract test fails if any of the three stops saying it.\n\nMINOR: 4.43.1 → 4.44.0.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
