    },
    "message": "feat(analytics): all-time totals that retention cannot prune, and uptime\n\nReported as \"the dashboard won't count past ~50,040 and hy3's lifetime\ntokens stopped rising\". Nothing was broken: REQUEST_LOG_MAX_ROWS caps\nstored rows at 50,000, prune runs every 100 inserts, and every figure in\nstats() is a SUM over that table. At the cap one row leaves for each one\nthat arrives, so the totals converge to a constant by construction. On\nthe reporter's live database that is a 5.5-day window over 1.7 GB.\n\nThere was no cumulative counter anywhere, so \"lifetime\" was really \"the\nlast 50,000 requests\" -- and nothing in the UI or the docs said so.\n\n- request_totals: one row per (day, provider, model), incremented at\n  flush and never pruned. Only ids that were not already stored are\n  folded in, because the insert is INSERT OR REPLACE and the counters\n  are add-only.\n- Seeded once from existing rows, before the writer accepts its first\n  flush so nothing is counted twice. 1.0s on a real 1.7 GB / 50k-row\n  database, no measurable file growth. History already pruned is gone.\n- server_sessions: the writer records when a server was actually\n  running. A flat stretch in the request chart is otherwise ambiguous --\n  no traffic and no server look identical -- which is exactly the second\n  question this report raised about an overnight gap.\n- Analytics gains an All time section, an explicit notice when the\n  stored rows are at the cap, and an uptime line under the series. The\n  gap threshold scales with the range so a restart does not cry wolf.\n- clear() erases the counters too; reporting millions of all-time\n  requests over an empty table would read as a bug.\n\nVerified against a consistent copy of the reporter's live database:\nwindowed frozen at 50,032 across 2,000 further inserts while all time\nwent 50,032 -> 52,032 and hy3 12,936 -> 14,936 requests. Driven in a\nreal browser: cards, table, notice and uptime line render from the real\n1.7 GB store, no console errors, contrast 7.30-17.97:1, and the new\ntable is the only analytics table that does not overflow at 492px.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
